======================================================================
PART 3: TOURNAMENT INTERPRETATION
======================================================================

  At the solution (x=2, y=10):
    x = 2     base of binary counting (graphs)
    y = 10    C(5,2) = number of tournament edges at n=5
    x+1 = 3   min_cycle of tournaments (Moon's theorem)
    x+2 = 4   n-1 where n=5

  The equation reads:
    (# labeled tournaments on 5 vertices) = (edges)^(min_cycle) + (n-1)!

  In words: the FULL binary space = POLYNOMIAL approximation + FACTORIAL correction

  The polynomial m^3 captures 1000/1024 = 97.7% of the space.
  The factorial 4! = 24 captures the remaining 2.3%.

  Structurally: 24 = |labeled copies of the unique Z_5-symmetric tournament|.
  These 24 tournaments are the most symmetric ones — one iso class with
  |Aut| = 5, giving n!/|Aut| = 120/5 = 24 labeled copies.

  Why is the non-Z_5 count exactly m^3?
  m = C(5,2) = 10. m^3 = 1000.
  non-Z_5 = |Aut|=1 count + |Aut|=3 count = 840 + 160 = 1000.

  Is 840 + 160 = 10^3 structural or coincidental?
  840 = 7 iso classes * 120 labeled/class (|Aut|=1 for each)
  160 = 4 iso classes * 40 labeled/class (|Aut|=3 for each)
  7 * 120 + 4 * 40 = 840 + 160 = 1000
  = 7 * 5! + 4 * 5!/3 = 5! * (7 + 4/3) = 120 * 25/3 = 1000
  Check: 120 * 25/3 = 3000/3 = 1000. ✓

  So: non-Z_5 count = 5! * (7 + 4/3) = 5! * 25/3
  And 5! * 25/3 = m^3 means: 120 * 25/3 = 10^3
  i.e., 25/3 = 1000/120 = 25/3. ✓ (tautology)

  The NUMBER 25/3 = (# trivial-Aut classes) + (# order-3-Aut classes)/3
  = 7 + 4/3 = 25/3
  This is specific to n=5. At n=4:
  n=4: non-Z_4 = 58, m^3 = 216. Equal? False
  NOT equal. The identity is specific to n=5.

